# Panda CSS Style Reuse

Two rules cohabit this file:

1. **Where styles live for atom consumers.** Our wrapped atoms (`Block`,
   `Text`, `Input`, `Textarea`, `Editor`, `Button`, `Dialog`, `Menu`,
   `Accordion`, `Panels`, `Slider`, `Progress`, `Video`, `Checkbox`,
   `Switch`, `Table`, `Tree`, `Kbd`, `RolodexCounter`, `RolodexText`,
   etc.) expose a typed Panda style-prop API. Consumer code MUST style
   atoms via that prop API, not by sticking a `className={css(...)}` next
   to them. See "The cascade contract" below.
2. **How Panda's static extractor sees literal style objects.** Panda
   only emits a CSS rule it can read at build time. Hide a literal
   behind a cross-file import and the rule silently disappears from the
   bundle with no warning. See "Panda extractor entry points" below.

Both rules pull in the same direction: keep the literal at the call site,
in the shape Panda's extractor expects.

## Atom-first — and stop-and-ask when an atom falls short

Default to atoms, not raw elements or CSS modules. When you write UI, reach
for the wrapped atoms first — `Block`, `Text`, `Button`, `Link`, `Input`,
`Menu`, `Dialog`, and friends — and style them through their Panda style-prop
API (plus the `css={{…}}` residue prop for the rare selector that has no prop).
Raw `<div>`/`<ul>`/`<a>` and `*.module.css` are the exception, not the reach.

Why: atoms already carry the motion wiring (they're framer-motion components),
the theme tokens, the focus/disabled/cascade behavior, and the typed prop API.
Every raw element or CSS module is a second styling system that drifts from the
atoms, skips the tokens, and can't be themed or animated the same way.

CSS modules are allowed only when atoms genuinely can't reach — e.g. a truly
global surface that must not depend on app-scoped atoms. When you keep a module,
**add a comment above it stating why an atom won't do.** No silent modules.

### Stop-and-ask is a hard rule

While writing UI, the moment you hit any of the following, **stop — do not work
around it, do not invent a parallel approach, do not quietly drop to a raw
element or a CSS module — and raise it with the user before going further:**

- An atom can't express what the design needs (a missing prop, variant, slot).
- You'd otherwise reach for a raw element or a CSS module to get the result.
- You spot a chance to extend an atom: a new style prop, a new slot, a new
  `_motion` hook, a new polymorphic `as` target.
- A new semantic token would express the case cleanly, or a selector you're
  reaching for will **recur across many components** and earns a shared custom
  condition in the config. (A selector that's specific to one component's markup
  is *not* this case — it stays inline in that component's `css`; see below.)
- The case really wants a whole new atom (a disclosure, a new menu, a link
  wrapper, …).

Name the concrete gap — which atom, which missing capability — and propose the
options (extend the atom / add a token or condition / new atom). Wait for the
decision, then build the agreed shape. The point is to keep pushing capability
*into* the atoms so the next feature inherits it, not to accumulate bespoke
styling around them.

Prefer porting or generalizing an existing atom over building a parallel one. If
an earlier UI already has the atom you need, bring it over (or generalize the
shared one) rather than re-solving it locally.

### Reach for the precise Panda prop, not `css` and not modules

Panda exposes far more as typed style props than plain CSS properties. Before you
drop to a `css={{…}}` residue (let alone a CSS module), check whether a prop or
condition already expresses it. The `css` residue is the *last* resort, reserved
for a selector that no prop or existing condition covers — typically a one-off
interaction tied to a single component's own markup. Inline that selector right
on the element; do **not** promote a one-off into a global config condition.

- **Conditions are props.** `_hover`, `_focusVisible`, `_active`, `_disabled`,
  `_selected`, `_checked`, `_open`, `_first`/`_last`/`_odd`/`_even`,
  `_groupHover`/`_peerHover`, and our custom `_dark`, `_notFocusWithin` are all
  style props. Nest them (`_hover={{ _after: {…} }}`) and combine at the property
  level (`bg={{ base: "surface-page", _hover: "surface-focus", _dark:
  "neutral.900" }}`).
- **Pseudo-elements are props.** `_before` / `_after` take a full style object
  (`_after={{ content: '""', position: "absolute", … }}`). Never hand-write
  `"&::after"` in a `css` residue.
- **Custom conditions in `panda.config` are for *recurring* selectors only.**
  Add a condition under `conditions.extend` (referenced as `_name`) when the same
  selector will be used across **many components** — like `_dark` or
  `_notFocusWithin`. The condition becomes global vocabulary, so it must earn that
  reach. A selector that's specific to one component's markup — keyed off that
  component's own data attributes, or a `:has()` relationship between elements it
  alone renders — is a **one-off**: it does not belong in the global config. Keep
  it inline in a `css={{…}}` prop on that element, where it lives next to the
  markup it depends on. `:has()`, `:not()`, and attribute selectors are valid in
  both places; the test is reuse, not syntax.

  ```tsx
  // ✅ One-off: this row's whole-body hover, suppressed while its own play
  // button is hovered. Tied to this component's markup → inline, not a config
  // condition.
  <Block
    as="li"
    data-selected={selected || undefined}
    css={{
      "&:not([data-selected]):hover:not(:has([data-play]:hover))": {
        backgroundColor: "surface-muted",
      },
    }}
  />

  // ❌ Don't mint a global condition for it.
  // panda.config: conditions.extend.rowHover = "&:hover:not(:has([data-play]:hover))"
  ```

  The config is still fair game to extend for genuinely shared vocabulary —
  recurring conditions, semantic tokens, utilities, breakpoints.
- **Responsive + container queries are props.** Use the breakpoint object/array
  syntax (`fontSize={{ base: "md", md: "lg" }}`) and container queries
  (`containerType` + `@/sm` keys) instead of `@media` strings in a residue.
- **Semantic tokens, not raw values.** Colors come from the `surface-*` /
  `text-*` / `border-*` / `accent` vocabulary so everything themes. Add a
  semantic token when a needed color isn't covered rather than pasting a hex.

### Vertical rhythm is the spacing system — use the tokens

`spacing`, `sizes`, and `radii` are one line-height rhythm scale
(`xs=0.25lh … sm=0.5lh … md=1lh … lg=1.5lh … xl=2lh … 2xl=2.5lh`), so padding,
gaps, widths, heights, and radii all land on the baseline. Style with these
tokens (`p="sm"`, `gap="md"`, `w="xl"`), and use the `flow` utility
(`& > * + *` margin) for rhythmic stacks. Arbitrary px (`6px`, `2px`, `13px`)
breaks the baseline grid — reach for a token, and if none fits, that's a signal
to discuss the scale, not to hardcode a pixel.

## The cascade contract (why prop API wins)

⚠️ **Critical empirical fact**: Panda's `cx()` in this codebase is
**naive string concatenation** — see
`packages/desktop/styled-system/css/cx.mjs`. It does NOT deduplicate
atomic classes by property. Two classes targeting the same property
both end up in the DOM and the cascade picks the winner by CSS source
order in the bundle — which is non-deterministic across builds.

It's `css(...args)` (NOT `cx`) that gives us deterministic override
behaviour. Per
`packages/desktop/styled-system/css/css.mjs:42`:

```js
export const css = (...styles) => cssFn(mergeCss(...styles))
```

`css(a, b, c)` deep-merges its arguments at the object level FIRST and
emits ONE class string for the merged result — later args win at the
property level. The atom's render must therefore funnel base styles +
consumer props through a SINGLE `css()` call so per-property overrides
win by object merge before any atomic class exists:

```ts
// atom (input.tsx)
const fieldRootStyles = {
  borderWidth: "1px",
  borderColor: "border-strong",
  _focusWithin: {
    borderColor: "blue.500",
    boxShadow: "0 0 0 3px var(--colors-blue-200)",
  },
} as const;

return (
  <motion.div
    className={cx(
      css(fieldRootStyles, styleProps, cssProp), // ← single css() call
      disabled ? _l.fieldRootDisabledClass : "", // ok: no property overlap
      className,                                  // ok: caller acknowledges race
    )}
  />
);
```

The consequence:

- `<Input border="0" outline="none" _focusWithin={{ boxShadow: "none" }}>`
  — the consumer's properties win the cascade DETERMINISTICALLY because
  they replaced the base values during object merge. The base
  `focusWithin:bd-c_blue.500` rule never even gets generated for this
  Input. No `!important`, no `&&` specificity tricks, no `cva`, no
  `.raw()`.
- `<Input className={css({ outline: "none" })}>` — `className` is still
  appended via `cx()` (it's a separate compiled class string), so it
  competes against `css(fieldRootStyles, styleProps, cssProp)` on the
  cascade-race terms above. ANY property the override needs to win for
  must be expressed as a style prop, not as `className`.

So: style atoms with their prop API. The override-via-className path
is structurally broken for property conflicts and has no chance of
winning deterministically.

### Forbidden: `cx(baseClass, css(styleProps))` for base+props composition

The pre-2026-05-22 shape — exporting a pre-computed
`fieldRootClass = css({...})` from `literals.ts`, then composing in the
atom with `cx(_l.fieldRootClass, css(styleProps, cssProp), className)`
— is the original source of the
Cmd+K-search-input-focus-ring saga. Both class strings end up in the
className, the cascade races on CSS source order, and `!important` /
`&&` / chrome variant props become the only "fixes". Don't reintroduce
it. If you see an atom doing this, refactor to the single-`css()`
pattern above.

## Default for consumer code: style props on the atom

```tsx
<Block grid gap="sm" bg="surface-panel" _hover={{ bg: "surface-muted" }}>
  …
</Block>

<Text fontSize="sm" color="text-muted">…</Text>

<Input border="0" outline="none" _focusWithin={{ boxShadow: "none" }} />
```

Mix prop overrides with `_hover`, `_focusWithin`, `_dark`, etc. nested
literals — all of those go through `splitCssProps` too.

## Allowed: `css={{ … }}` JSX prop for residue

When some keys have no prop equivalent (unscoped descendant selectors,
weird pseudo-elements, `@supports` blocks, experimental properties like
`cornerShape`), keep ONLY those keys in a `css={{...}}` prop. Hoist the
rest into named props.

```tsx
<Block
  grid
  gap="sm"
  position="relative"
  isolation="isolate"
  css={{
    "&::after": { content: '""', /* … */ },
    "&[data-pill-owner='pointer'] [data-open-pill]": { opacity: 1 },
  }}
/>
```

`css={{...}}` is NOT the default — if every key is prop-able, use props.

### Litmus before each `css={{}}` key: is it secretly a prop?

The recurring mistake is dumping ordinary properties and standard states into
`css={{}}` while *also* setting `rounded` / `cursor` / `userSelect` as props on
the same element — an inconsistency that signals the `css` block wasn't needed.
A key belongs in `css` ONLY if it has no prop or condition form. These all have
a prop form and must NOT appear as `css` keys:

- **Plain properties**: `outline`, `boxShadow`, `background`, `opacity`,
  `cursor`, `userSelect`, `borderRadius` (→ `rounded`). Set them as props.
- **Standard states**: `&:hover`, `&:focus-visible`, `&:active`, `&:disabled`,
  `&::before`, `&::after` → `_hover`, `_focusVisible`, `_active`, `_disabled`,
  `_before`, `_after` (each takes a full style object).
- **A dynamic value** the element computes (a selected/drop-target boxShadow):
  make it a conditional *prop* — `boxShadow={isTarget ? "…" : undefined}` — not
  a `css` key.

```tsx
// ❌ properties + standard states stuffed into css, next to real props
<Block
  rounded="sm"
  userSelect="none"
  css={{
    outline: "none",
    "&:focus-visible": { boxShadow: "inset 0 0 0 2px var(--colors-blue-300)" },
    "&:hover": { backgroundColor: "surface-focus" },
  }}
/>

// ✅ every one of those is a prop
<Block
  rounded="sm"
  userSelect="none"
  outline="none"
  _focusVisible={{ boxShadow: "inset 0 0 0 2px var(--colors-blue-300)" }}
  _hover={{ bg: "surface-focus" }}
/>
```

What genuinely stays in `css={{}}`: a selector with no prop form — a markup-
specific `:not([data-selected]):hover:not(:has(...))` one-off, an unscoped
descendant selector, a `@supports` block, an experimental property.

## Allowed: `className={css({...})}` on raw HTML wrappers (atom internals)

Raw HTML elements (`<div>`, `<span>`, `<svg>`, `<button>`) have no
Panda prop API, so atom internals legitimately reach for
`className={css({...})}`. This is the pattern in
`atoms/blocks/fields/input.tsx`, `atoms/icon/section-error/index.tsx`,
etc.

```tsx
// inside an atom: wrapping a raw <span>, no prop API exists.
<span className={css({ display: "grid", placeItems: "center" })}>{start}</span>
```

This is the ONLY remaining home for `className={css(...)}`. Consumer
code that imports an atom must not use this shape.

### Trap: a shared raw-DOM class is not a license to `className` an atom

A `css()` class declared for a legitimate raw-DOM use — e.g. a pill built
with `document.createElement`, which has no prop API — is **not** reusable
as `className` on an atom that renders the same look. The instant the same
visual is a `<Block as="span">`, port the literal onto the atom as style
props. Duplicating the literal at that second call site is expected and
correct; it is **not** a DRY violation. "One source of truth" / "they
should look identical" is never a reason to put a `className` on an atom —
the prop API is the contract, and `className` on an atom loses the cascade
race besides (see "The cascade contract").

```tsx
// ❌ borrowing the raw-DOM pill class onto an atom
<Block as="span" className={pillClassName}>{label}</Block>

// ✅ same look, expressed as props (hue rides in via the --pill-hue var)
<Block
  as="span"
  display="inline-flex"
  alignItems="center"
  borderRadius="xs"
  paddingInline="0.4375rem"
  paddingBlock="0.125rem"
  fontSize="0.8125rem"
  fontWeight={500}
  whiteSpace="nowrap"
  bg="color-mix(in srgb, var(--pill-hue) 18%, transparent)"
  color="color-mix(in srgb, var(--pill-hue) 72%, var(--colors-text-primary))"
  style={{ ["--pill-hue" as string]: `var(--colors-${color}-500)` }}
>
  {label}
</Block>
```

Default away from `className` on every atom, hard. If you ever believe an
atom genuinely cannot express the styling without one, that is the
stop-and-ask trigger above — raise the concrete gap with the user before
reaching for `className`. Never default to it silently.

## Allowed: pre-computed class string in `literals.ts` for ADDITIVE chrome

A pre-computed class via `css({...})` in `literals.ts` is fine when the
class adds properties that DO NOT overlap with the atom's base styles
or with what consumers are expected to override — e.g.
`fieldRootDisabledClass` only sets `cursor: not-allowed` and `opacity`.
Composing via `cx(mergedCssCall, additiveClass)` is safe here because
there's no property race.

```ts
// literals.ts — additive only
export const fieldRootDisabledClass = css({
  cursor: "not-allowed",
  opacity: 0.6,
});
```

The literal MUST live inline inside the `css({...})` call — exporting
`as const` then `css(imported)` silently drops nested conditions
(Panda's static extractor can't follow cross-file references for nested
selectors like `_focusWithin`).

For the atom's BASE chrome — the styles consumers may override via
props — the literal goes inline in the atom file as a plain object,
NOT a pre-computed class. See "The cascade contract" above for why.

## Forbidden shapes

```tsx
// ❌ className={css(...)} on an atom — fights the atom's base class at
//    equal specificity and loses the declaration-order race.
<Block className={css({ display: "grid", _hover: { bg: "surface-muted" } })} />

// ❌ Spreading an imported style object — Panda's static extractor can't
//    see the keys, so nested conditions disappear from the bundle.
<Block {...importedStyleObject} />
className={css(importedStyleObject)}

// ❌ css={{...}} when every key is a Panda style prop — use named props.
<Text css={{ fontSize: "sm", color: "text-muted" }}>…</Text>

// ❌ Recipes / cva / sva / styled.* — adds a vocabulary we don't
//    maintain. Two pre-computed classes + cx() covers every variant case.
import { cva } from "panda/css"; // never
```

## Dynamic style-prop values: inline the literals, or use raw `style={{}}`

Panda reads your source **as text at build time** and emits a CSS rule for
every literal value it sees on a style prop. It never runs your code. So a
style prop fed a **runtime variable** gets **no rule for the actual value** —
and there is **no error**. The prop silently falls back to the CSS default.

```tsx
// ❌ Dynamic value — Panda sees a variable, not "1 / 4", so it emits nothing.
//    At runtime grid-column has no rule → falls back to `auto` → the element
//    auto-flows into a leftover sliver. (This was a real cinema-stage bug.)
const span = open ? "1 / 4" : "1 / 2";
<Block gridColumn={span} />
```

**The "works by accident" trap.** The value only renders if that *exact*
literal happens to appear statically somewhere else in the source (Panda
generated the class for that other site, and your runtime string matches it).
`"1 / 2"` "worked" only because another component wrote `gridColumn="1 / 2"`
literally; `"1 / 4"` appeared nowhere, so it died. Never rely on this.

Two correct shapes, by how open-ended the value is:

```tsx
// ✅ Small known set → inline the literals at the prop. Panda statically reads
//    BOTH ternary branches and emits a rule for each. Stays in the token system.
<Block gridColumn={open ? "1 / 4" : "1 / 2"} />

// ✅ Open-ended / continuous value (computed px, interpolated, animated motion
//    value) → write raw inline style. It bypasses Panda's static extractor
//    entirely and always applies. This is the right tool when the value can't
//    be enumerated as literals.
<Block style={{ gridColumn: computedSpan }} />
<Block _motion={{ style: { gridTemplateColumns: columnsMotionValue } }} />
```

Rule of thumb: if you can list every possible value, inline them as literals so
Panda owns them; if you can't, reach for raw `style={{}}` (or `_motion.style`
for motion values). Never hand a Panda style prop a bare variable and hope.

Verify with the DevTools grep in "How to verify a rule is actually present" —
an empty result means Panda never saw your value.

## Cross-file references that ARE safe

These don't trigger the extractor problem:

- Importing **token strings** (`"blue.500"`, `"surface-panel"`, `"xs"`) —
  the consumer still pastes them into an inline literal.
- Importing **class strings** produced by a `css({...})` call at the
  definition site (Panda already extracted the literal there).
- Importing **numbers, durations, transitions for `framer-motion`**, etc.
  that never reach Panda.

## Exception: panda recipes / cva are forbidden

This repo does not use `cva`, `sva`, `styled.*`, or `panda.config.ts`
recipes. Two pre-computed `css({...})` class strings + `cx()` express
every variant case we need without the extra vocabulary.

## How to verify a rule is actually present

In DevTools (or `cdp Runtime.evaluate` on `document.styleSheets`), grep
the rules for the literal value:

```js
[...document.styleSheets].flatMap(s => { try { return [...s.cssRules] } catch { return [] } })
  .map(r => r.cssText)
  .filter(t => t.includes("var(--colors-blue-200)") && t.includes("focus-within"))
```

If the array is empty, Panda didn't see the literal — convert per this
rule.

## Why this rule lives here, not in code

oxlint can't easily enforce this and we don't want to add ESLint just
for one rule. This file is the source of truth; PR review + agent
attention is the enforcement mechanism. When in doubt: prop API on
atoms, inline literal at the call site for residue.
